"""Original nutrition drill; the first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
A major function of carbohydrate in the diet is to|provide energy|form the only source of all vitamins|replace the need for water|guarantee prevention of every illness|Carbohydrates are an important source of energy.
Protein provides|amino acids needed for growth and tissue maintenance|only water|only dietary fibre|only sodium chloride|Protein supplies amino acids used in many functions, including growth and repair.
Fat can support|energy supply and absorption of fat-soluble vitamins|only the formation of oxygen|complete immunity from disease|replacement of all other nutrients|Fat provides energy and supports absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.
Vitamins and minerals are|needed in smaller amounts for many body functions|unnecessary because only energy matters|identical to dietary water|always required in unlimited amounts|Micronutrients are needed in relatively small quantities but have important functions.
Iron is important in relation to|haemoglobin formation|the relay exchange zone|bone-to-bone attachment alone|a competition draw|Iron contributes to formation of haemoglobin.
Iodine is needed for production of|thyroid hormones|the baton in a relay|synovial fluid alone|oxygen in the lungs|Iodine is needed for thyroid hormone production.
Calcium contributes to|bones and teeth|only a match score|only a game's rules|the production of a shuttlecock|Calcium contributes to bone and tooth health among other functions.
Dietary fibre can support|digestive health|a guarantee that no illness will occur|the replacement of drinking water|a diagnosis by itself|Fibre can support digestive health, but it is not a substitute for water or medical care.
Water has roles including|transport and temperature regulation|only replacing bone|eliminating the need for all food|providing a complete diagnosis|Water supports normal functions including transport and temperature regulation.
Which food can contribute protein as well as other nutrients?|beans|a food label alone|clean drinking water as a protein source|table salt as the sole protein source|Beans can provide protein, carbohydrate and fibre.
A food described as a protein source|may also contain carbohydrate, fat or other nutrients|contains protein and nothing else in every case|cannot be part of a varied diet|is automatically a treatment|Foods commonly contain mixtures of nutrients.
Which is an example of a starchy staple?|yam|table salt|drinking water|iodised salt|Yam is a starchy root that supplies carbohydrate.
Dietary needs can vary with|age, activity, health and individual circumstances|only the name of a meal|only a person's initials|one universal target regardless of circumstance|Needs vary with growth, activity, health and other factors.
Food choices can be affected by|availability, cost, culture and preference|knowledge alone in every case|a sports score alone|only the colour of a plate|Access, resources and culture can influence choices.
A varied dietary pattern is assessed most usefully|over time and in context|from one food alone|only from a person's appearance|without considering access|Overall dietary patterns and individual circumstances matter.
Malnutrition includes|undernutrition and micronutrient deficiencies or excesses|only one particular body size|only a single missed meal|only infection|Malnutrition covers several forms, including undernutrition and micronutrient problems.
A person's appearance alone|does not establish a complete nutritional diagnosis|always reveals every deficiency|replaces a qualified assessment|proves the cause of illness|Appearance alone is not sufficient for a complete diagnosis.
The WHO adult recommendation for fruit and vegetables includes|at least 400 grams daily|exactly 40 grams daily for every person|no vegetables|only starchy roots|The cited WHO adult guidance recommends at least 400 grams of fruit and vegetables daily.
In the WHO 400-gram fruit-and-vegetable recommendation, cassava is|a starchy root excluded from that count|always counted as a fruit|counted as drinking water|the only food needed|WHO excludes starchy roots such as cassava from this fruit-and-vegetable recommendation.
The cited adult WHO recommendation for salt is|less than 5 grams per day|at least 50 grams per day|no need to consider salt|identical to a prescription for every child|The cited WHO population guidance recommends less than 5 grams of salt daily for adults.
Population-level nutrition guidance|may need individual adaptation and professional advice|is a precise prescription for every individual|replaces all medical assessment|means age and health do not matter|Recommendations may require adjustment for individual circumstances.
A label states 6 g per serving and two servings per packet. The whole packet contains|12 g|3 g|6 g|8 g|Multiply six grams per serving by two servings to get twelve grams.
A food label states 5 g per serving and four servings per packet. Three servings contain|15 g|5 g|9 g|20 g|Three servings at five grams each provide fifteen grams.
The first step in interpreting an amount on a food label is to|check the serving size and quantity consumed|assume the amount applies to the whole packet|ignore the units|infer a medical diagnosis|The serving size is needed before calculating the amount consumed.
Food safety includes|keeping clean, separating raw food and cooking thoroughly|using the same unwashed surface for all foods|storing all perishable food without regard to temperature|assuming appearance proves safety|Clean handling, separation and adequate cooking reduce food-safety risks.
Cross-contamination can occur when|an unwashed knife used for raw meat is used on ready-to-eat food|clean equipment is kept separate|safe water is used for washing|hands are cleaned before handling food|Microorganisms can transfer from raw food through contaminated equipment.
After cutting raw meat, the safest general action before preparing a salad is to|clean equipment and separate raw from ready-to-eat food|reuse the unwashed equipment|assume raw meat cannot contaminate surfaces|ignore hand hygiene|Cleaning and separation reduce the risk of transfer.
A food that looks and smells normal|can still be unsafe|is guaranteed to be sterile|cannot contain harmful microorganisms|always needs no safe handling|Appearance and smell do not reliably reveal all hazards.
Safe food preparation should use|safe water and suitable ingredients|contaminated water whenever convenient|unwashed equipment after raw meat|no attention to storage|Safe water and ingredients are part of food-safety practice.
Food should be stored|at temperatures appropriate to the food and local guidance|at any temperature indefinitely|without regard to whether it is perishable|only according to its package colour|Temperature and storage time affect the safety of perishable foods.
Drinking water needs|vary with the person, environment and activity|are identical for everyone in all circumstances|can always be established by a textbook alone|are unrelated to exertion|Fluid requirements vary and individual medical circumstances can matter.
Urine colour or thirst|may offer clues but is not a diagnosis by itself|provides a complete medical diagnosis|means all individuals have identical fluid needs|replaces assessment of concerning symptoms|Simple clues do not replace individual assessment.
Confusion or other severe symptoms with suspected dehydration|can require prompt clinical care|should always be ignored|can be diagnosed from the colour of a cup|must be treated by copying another person's plan|Concerning symptoms warrant appropriate prompt assessment.
Which statement about food groups is most accurate?|Many foods contain more than one nutrient|Every food contains exactly one nutrient|A single food automatically supplies all needs|All foods have identical composition|Foods contain mixtures, so categories are simplifications.
Choosing foods with unsaturated fats where appropriate|can be part of a healthy dietary pattern|means fat has no energy|guarantees no health condition|removes the need for variety|Fats differ in composition; overall dietary patterns and individual needs matter.
Industrially produced trans fats should generally be|limited in a healthy dietary pattern|treated as essential in unlimited quantities|assumed identical to drinking water|used to replace every other nutrient|WHO advises limiting industrially produced trans fats.
A lesson on nutrition that ignores food affordability|may overlook an important barrier to dietary choices|automatically solves access problems|proves that all people have identical options|establishes a medical diagnosis|Resources and food availability affect practical choices.
A single meal|does not by itself fully determine a person's overall diet|proves a complete diagnosis|establishes all nutrient needs|reveals every health condition|An overall dietary pattern is considered over time and in context.
If a label gives 8 g per serving and a person eats half a serving, the amount is|4 g|8 g|12 g|16 g|Half of eight grams is four grams.
A claim that one product cures every nutritional problem should be|checked against reliable evidence and individual advice|accepted without evidence|used to replace prescribed care automatically|assumed correct because it is advertised|Broad cure claims require evidence and do not replace individual assessment.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
